{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q010_rossmann_family_function_divergence","answer":"**Verified SCOPe facts (release 2.08, current stable release).** All three named groups are sibling *families* under one superfamily. Alcohol dehydrogenase-like, C-terminal domain is family c.2.1.1 (sunid 51736); CoA-binding domain is family c.2.1.8 (sunid 51900); Potassium channel NAD-binding domain is family c.2.1.9 (sunid 63944). Their common parent is superfamily c.2.1 'NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold domains' (sunid 51735), which sits in fold c.2 'NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold domains' (sunid 51734) in class c, alpha and beta proteins (a/b). In 2.08 fold c.2 contains only this one superfamily, and c.2.1 contains 13 families (c.2.1.1 through c.2.1.13 except c.2.1.10, plus an 'automated matches' family c.2.1.0). The fold node comment describes the core as 3 layers a/b/a with a parallel 6-stranded beta-sheet, strand order 321456, and notes that the nucleotide-binding modes of this and the next two folds/superfamilies are similar. Family-level comments: c.2.1.1 says 'N-terminal all-beta domain defines family' (i.e. the family is delimited by the partner domain in the chain, not by the Rossmann domain itself); c.2.1.9 is 'automatically mapped to Pfam 02254' (PF02254, the TrkA_N / KTN domain); c.2.1.8 has no comment. The homology sections for all three families and for the superfamily report homology_implied_by_level = true with no warnings, heterogeneity flags, or repeat annotations.

**Interpretation.** The deepest shared homology-bearing level among the three is the superfamily, not the family. SCOPe therefore asserts probable common evolutionary origin for the alcohol-dehydrogenase NAD-binding domains, the CoA-binding domains (e.g. of succinyl-CoA synthetase-type ligases), and the TrkA/KTN-type potassium-channel regulatory domains, inferred from shared Rossmann topology plus the conserved dinucleotide-binding mode, despite sequence similarity that is generally too weak for family-level grouping. Placement in separate families means SCOPe does *not* claim the closer relationship (clear sequence similarity or near-identical function and structure) that family membership would imply. A biologist should read this as: these are remote homologs within one nucleotide-binding superfamily that have diverged substantially in sequence and in biological role, from catalytic NAD(P)-dependent oxidoreduction (c.2.1.1) to CoA/nucleotide binding in ligases (c.2.1.8) to ligand-sensing gating domains in ion channels (c.2.1.9), none of which is implied by the shared superfamily label. The superfamily name 'NAD(P)-binding' is a historical descriptor, not a guarantee that every member binds NAD(P) or binds it the same way.

**Limits and unsafe transfers.** (1) Function must not be transferred across these families: superfamily membership does not establish that the CoA-binding or K-channel domains catalyse a dehydrogenase reaction, bind NAD(P) with the same affinity or specificity, or share cofactor-dependence; ligand identity, catalytic residues, and regulatory roles require ligand-bound structures, biochemical assays, or conserved-motif analysis at equivalent structural positions. (2) The c.2.1.1 comment shows that this family is defined by a *different* domain (the N-terminal all-beta catalytic domain of medium-chain alcohol dehydrogenases, classified elsewhere), so domain architecture, not Rossmann-domain features alone, delimits that family; comparing whole chains would mix in that non-homologous partner domain. (3) The c.2.1.9 family is annotated as automatically mapped to Pfam PF02254; this is provenance, not a quality judgment, but a reader relying on the Pfam cross-reference should check the mapping for individual domains. (4) Shared superfamily does not make any member an interchangeable structural template or homology-modelling target for another family: core topology is conserved but loop, insertion, oligomerisation, and binding-site details are family- or protein-specific, and a specific modelling claim needs an inspected alignment. (5) Because fold c.2 contains only superfamily c.2.1 in 2.08, fold-level and superfamily-level statements coincide here; the fold comment about similar nucleotide-binding modes in the next two folds (c.3, c.4) is a structural observation and is not a SCOPe homology assertion, so those folds should not be described as homologous or as non-homologous to c.2.1 on SCOPe evidence alone. (6) Statements about how these families were classified in earlier SCOP releases, or about particular phylogenetic scenarios of divergence, were not verified from the retrieved 2.08 data.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.2.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.2.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.2.1/children?limit=40","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51734","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51734/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51734/children?limit=10"]}]}
